---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/Prose.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { LINKS, EMAIL, CADKIT_VERSION, repoFile } from '../../config';

const title = 'Security';
const description =
	'cadkit treats every drawing as hostile input. How the readers are hardened, how to report a vulnerability privately, and which versions receive fixes.';
---

<PageLayout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Security' }]} eyebrow="Security policy">
	<div class="ck-container ck-security">
		<aside class="ck-report" aria-labelledby="report">
			<h2 id="report">Report a vulnerability</h2>
			<p>Report privately. Do not open a public issue.</p>
			<ul class="ck-grips">
				<li>
					<a href={LINKS.securityAdvisory}>GitHub private vulnerability reporting</a> (preferred): the repository's
					Security tab, “Report a vulnerability”.
				</li>
				<li>E-mail <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL.security}`}>{EMAIL.security}</a>.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="ck-small">
				Machine-readable contact: <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">/.well-known/security.txt</a> (RFC 9116).
			</p>
		</aside>

		<Prose>
			<h2 id="stance">Every input is untrusted</h2>
			<p>
				cadkit parses binary and XML formats (DWG, DGN V7 and V8, DXF and CityGML 2.0) that routinely arrive from
				third parties, so every file is treated as hostile:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Library code must not panic on any input. The workspace lints deny <code>unwrap</code>,{' '}
					<code>expect</code>, <code>panic!</code> and unchecked slice indexing.
				</li>
				<li>
					Every size, count and offset read from a file is checked against <code>ReadOptions::limits</code> (input
					size, decompressed size, object count, nesting depth, string length, vertex count) before memory is
					allocated or a loop runs. Recursion is bounded, and XML parsing disables external entities and network
					resolution.
				</li>
				<li>
					Readers fail only when no useful document can be built; recoverable damage becomes a warning on the
					document.
				</li>
				<li>
					The readers are pure Rust. <code>unsafe</code> appears only in the binding crates (Python, WebAssembly,
					C), each block with a written safety argument. The C API catches panics at the boundary and null-checks
					every pointer.
				</li>
				<li>Readers are fuzzed in CI, and public samples are truncated and bit-flipped in tests.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2 id="hardening">Processing files you did not create</h2>
			<p>
				The default limits are generous. For untrusted files, lower them through <code>ReadOptions</code> (Rust) or{' '}
				<code>cadkit_options</code> (C). The Python, WebAssembly and CLI interfaces are gaining limit options and may
				not expose all of them yet; check their documentation. In every case, run conversions of untrusted files in a
				sandboxed process with memory and time limits: parsing a format is not a security boundary by itself.
			</p>

			<h2 id="what-to-send">What to include in a report</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>The affected version or commit.</li>
				<li>A minimal input file, or the code that generates it.</li>
				<li>What you observed: panic message, memory growth, hang or crash.</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout variant="caution">
				<p>
					Do not send drawings that contain confidential data. A reduced or synthetic file that triggers the same
					behaviour is preferred.
				</p>
			</Callout>
			<p>
				You can expect an acknowledgement within a few days and a fix or mitigation plan after triage. Reporters are
				credited in the changelog unless they prefer to stay anonymous.
			</p>

			<h2 id="supported-versions">Supported versions</h2>
			<p>
				While cadkit is before 1.0, only the latest release (currently <code>{CADKIT_VERSION}</code>) and the{' '}
				<code>main</code> branch receive fixes. Upgrade to the latest release before reporting if you can.
			</p>

			<h2 id="out-of-scope">Out of scope</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Vulnerabilities in other software that consumes cadkit output, for example a viewer that renders generated
					SVG without sanitising text.
				</li>
				<li>Denial of service by inputs that are large but within the configured limits.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The policy is maintained in <a href={repoFile('SECURITY.md')}>SECURITY.md</a>; this page summarises it.
			</p>
		</Prose>
	</div>
</PageLayout>

<style>
	.ck-security {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--ck-space-8);
		align-items: start;
	}
	@media (min-width: 64rem) {
		.ck-security {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 45rem) minmax(0, 22rem);
		}
		.ck-report {
			grid-column: 2;
			grid-row: 1;
		}
	}
	.ck-report {
		position: relative;
		padding: var(--ck-space-6);
		border: 1px solid var(--ck-border);
		border-top: 2px solid var(--ck-accent);
		background: var(--ck-surface);
	}
	.ck-report h2 {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--sl-text-xl);
	}
	.ck-report p {
		margin: var(--ck-space-2) 0 var(--ck-space-4);
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		color: var(--ck-muted);
	}
	.ck-report .ck-grips {
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
	}
	.ck-report .ck-small {
		margin: var(--ck-space-4) 0 0;
		font-size: var(--sl-text-xs);
	}
</style>
